<h1>Brandon Johnson</h1>
<p>Mayor of Chicago since 2023. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>Mayor since May 2023, seeking a second term. A former CPS teacher, Chicago Teachers Union organizer, and Cook County commissioner; 50, lives in Austin, married with three children, one of ten siblings. Filed 33,000 signatures on October 23.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.brandonforchicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (brandonforchicago.com)</a></p>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Friends of Brandon Johnson</li>
<li>Raised: $123K</li>
<li>Spent: $858K</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $379K</li>
<li>Raised July 1 to Sept. 30, 2026; spent Jan. 1 to Sept. 30, 2026. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=34198" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>13.6% of likely voters in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 16 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>What they would do</h2>
<ul>
<li>Make Chicago the safest and most affordable big city in the country by investing in working people. <q>We are going to be the safest, most affordable big city in America by investing in working people.</q> (<a href="https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/local/who-is-running-for-chicago-mayor-what-to-know-as-nominating-period-ends/4008071/" rel="nofollow noopener">NBC Chicago, Oct. 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Reopen every closed public school and every closed mental health clinic. <q>We will not be satisfied until every mental health clinic is reopened.</q> (<a href="https://thetriibe.com/2026/09/read-mayor-brandon-johnsons-full-speech-from-his-reelection-campaign-launch-at-dusable-museum/" rel="nofollow noopener">Reelection launch speech, Sept. 13, 2026 (The TRiiBE)</a>)</li>
<li>Keep building housing with city money instead of waiting on Washington. <q>We have built a model for creating housing that does not wait on Washington to save us.</q> (<a href="https://thetriibe.com/2026/09/read-mayor-brandon-johnsons-full-speech-from-his-reelection-campaign-launch-at-dusable-museum/" rel="nofollow noopener">Reelection launch speech, Sept. 13, 2026 (The TRiiBE)</a>)</li>
<li>Points to his executive order creating an Office of Gun Violence Prevention as the model for driving violence down. <q>It&#39;s my executive order that created an Office of Gun Violence Prevention.</q> (<a href="https://thetriibe.com/2026/09/read-mayor-brandon-johnsons-full-speech-from-his-reelection-campaign-launch-at-dusable-museum/" rel="nofollow noopener">Reelection launch speech, Sept. 13, 2026 (The TRiiBE)</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>Main points</h3>
<p>Safety and affordability: A second term built on investing in working people, with the goal of the safest, most affordable big city in America.</p>
<p>Schools and clinics: Reopen every closed public school and every closed mental health clinic, and keep sending TIF surplus money to the schools.</p>
<p>Housing and violence: Continue the city-funded housing programs of his first term and the Office of Gun Violence Prevention he created.</p>
<h3>Next to the others</h3>
<p>Only Johnson: Runs on a record in the office; everyone else runs against it.</p>
<p>Taxes: Johnson wants the ultra-rich to pay more; Giannoulias, Holberg, Kitover, and Kelly would hold or lower property taxes, and Quigley calls raising taxes the last option.</p>
<p>Mental health: Johnson and Quigley would reopen the neighborhood clinics.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Says the city should not balance its budget on working people and that the very rich should pay more in taxes; counts the TIF surplus sent to the public schools as his way of finding money.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>We will not be satisfied until the ultra-rich finally pay their fair share in taxes.</q> (<a href="https://abc7chicago.com/post/chicago-mayor-brandon-johnson-make-special-announcement-sunday/19826432/" rel="nofollow noopener">ABC7 Chicago, Sept. 13, 2026</a>)</li>
<li><q>When times get hard, we do not balance the budget off the backs of working people.</q> (<a href="https://thetriibe.com/2026/09/read-mayor-brandon-johnsons-full-speech-from-his-reelection-campaign-launch-at-dusable-museum/" rel="nofollow noopener">Reelection launch speech, Sept. 13, 2026 (The TRiiBE)</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>The first mayor from the West Side in nearly a century, and an all-conference defensive lineman at Elgin High. (<a href="https://news.wttw.com/2023/04/14/touring-chicago-s-austin-community-mayor-elect-brandon-johnson" rel="nofollow noopener">news.wttw.com</a>)</li>
<li>Opened his reelection speech with a joke that he would hurry because it was already as long as a City Council meeting. (<a href="https://thetriibe.com/2026/09/read-mayor-brandon-johnsons-full-speech-from-his-reelection-campaign-launch-at-dusable-museum/" rel="nofollow noopener">thetriibe.com</a>)</li>
</ul>
